rsus 1987, 1990, 2000-02, 2008-09, 2020 flight-to-quality); Fermi's shock-type weighting gives 0.43; Scout's gives 0.35. These converge, so I take 0.39 and then shade DOWN to 0.36 for the one conditioning correction all three seats independently identify and which I regard as the sharpest finding in this effort: the MODAL generator of a >=25% drawdown in this specific window is the AI capex bust (AI-Q4 at 66% raw), and a capex bust is DISINFLATIONARY, so bonds rally hard and conjunct B FAILS. The question is quietly asking for two things from opposite shock families. LR on that conditioning: P(locked prior set skewing the shock mix toward a disinflationary bust | B2 conditional below the historical 38%) / P(same | at or above) = 0.65/0.30 = 2.2, above the bar, but it only earns a small shade because the historical 3-of-8 already contains inflationary and fiscal episodes. Over the decade, Poisson-compounding: 1 - exp(-1.4 x 0.36) = 0.39; I take B = 0.38.\n\nCORRELATION: strongly positive, +0.45 to +0.50 across all three seats, and structurally sound — a decade averaging MOVE >=105 IS the fiscal-dominance regime in which sovereigns stop hedging equities. Multiplicative ratio 1.42 (I cap below the seats' 1.5 because B is already elevated). P(B|A) = 0.38 x 1.42 = 0.54.\n\nMULTIPLICATION: 0.33 x 0.54 = 0.178 -> 18.\n\nDIRECTION: MODERATED downward by -4 points (22 -> 18). Conjunct A fell (0.35 -> 0.33) on a signed but sub-2:1 scaling argument; conjunct B fell slightly (0.40 -> 0.38) on the shock-mix conditioning. Both moves are small BY DESIGN, because the evidence base for A is the least verifiable in the exercise and a Bayesian who moves far on it is doing the thing this seat exists to prevent. Net of the two conjuncts I land exactly on Fermi's 18 by a different route.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:55.836Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist fa